<h1>MD5 vs SHA-256: Which Hash Should You Use?</h1>
<p>MD5 and SHA-256 are both hash functions, but they should not be treated as equal choices for modern work. MD5 produces a 128-bit digest. SHA-256 produces a 256-bit digest and belongs to the SHA-2 family defined by the Secure Hash Standard. For new security-sensitive systems, SHA-256 is the safer and more current default.</p>
<p>The main reason is not only length. A longer digest gives more room against brute-force and collision attacks, but the real issue is the security history of the algorithms. MD5 has known collision weaknesses and should not be used for digital signatures, certificates, password storage, or integrity checks where an attacker can choose inputs. <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6151">RFC 6151</a> gives official updated security considerations for MD5.</p>
<p>SHA-256 is specified in NIST's Secure Hash Standard, <a href="https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/180-4/upd1/final">FIPS 180-4</a>. It is widely supported across programming languages, command-line tools, package managers, cloud systems, and security products. That wide support makes it a practical default when you need a file hash, API signing component, or integrity value in a current system.</p>
<p>There are still places where MD5 appears. Older software repositories, legacy databases, firmware pages, and archived tutorials may publish MD5 checksums. If you are maintaining a system that already expects MD5, you may need to calculate MD5 for compatibility. That does not mean you should choose MD5 for a new workflow. Compatibility and best practice are different questions.</p>
<p>For file verification, SHA-256 is usually the better choice when you control the hash being published. For example, if your team shares internal release files, publish SHA-256 checksums. If you receive only an MD5 checksum from an old vendor, use it as a basic accidental-corruption check, but do not treat it as strong proof against tampering.</p>
<p>For passwords, neither plain MD5 nor plain SHA-256 is the right answer. Passwords need slow, salted, adaptive hashing. OWASP recommends purpose-built password hashing algorithms such as Argon2id, bcrypt, scrypt, or PBKDF2 depending on requirements. This point matters because many beginner tutorials compare MD5 and SHA-256 as if switching to SHA-256 alone fixes password storage. It does not.</p>
<p>Performance also matters. MD5 is fast, which helped make it popular. But speed is a weakness when protecting passwords because attackers can test guesses quickly. SHA-256 is also fast compared with password hashing algorithms. Fast hashes are useful for files and data integrity. They are not enough for user password databases.</p>
<p>When deciding between the two, ask who controls the input. If only accidental damage is realistic, MD5 may still catch many ordinary mistakes. If an attacker could create or swap files, MD5 is the wrong choice. This is why the same algorithm may be tolerable in an old backup comparison but unacceptable in a software update trust chain.</p>
<p>A practical rule is simple. Use SHA-256 for new file checksums and general-purpose hashing. Use MD5 only when you need compatibility with existing MD5 data or older tools. Use bcrypt, Argon2id, scrypt, or PBKDF2 for passwords. If you need a refresher on where MD5 still fits, read <a href="/what-is-md5-hashing.html">What Is MD5 Hashing</a> first, then treat SHA-256 as the modern baseline for most non-password hash tasks.</p>
